## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of bit pattern encoding, branchless 64-bit manipulation, and mask-driven bit dilation techniques. It evaluates consistency across endianness and fixed-width arithmetic skills.

## Key Concepts

- Branchless bit manipulation
- Fixed-width masks for progressive dilation
- 64-bit hexadecimal mask constants
- Bit-level reasoning and positional mapping

## Code Example

```c
uint64_t spread32to64(uint32_t x) {
    uint64_t y = x;

    // Progressive dilation using fixed masks
    y = (y | (y << 16)) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL;
    y = (y | (y << 8))  & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL;
    y = (y | (y << 4))  & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
    y = (y | (y << 2))  & 0x3333333333333333ULL;
    y = (y | (y << 1))  & 0x5555555555555555ULL;

    // Duplicate each bit to adjacent position
    return y | (y << 1);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to perform endianness reversal across a 128-bit value with no branches.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit layout via hi/lo; byte-level reversal; pure bit tricks.
- 64-bit byte swap implementation using shifts/masks.
- Branchless data movement across two halves.

## Code Example
```c
static inline uint64_t bswap64(uint64_t x){
  x = ((x & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL) << 32) | ((x & 0xFFFFFFFF00000000ULL) >> 32);
  x = ((x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16) | ((x & 0xFFFF0000FFFF0000ULL) >> 16);
  x = ((x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8)  | ((x & 0xFF00FF00FF00FF00ULL) >> 8);
  return x;
}
void swapEndianness128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo){
  *out_hi = bswap64(lo);
  *out_lo = bswap64(hi);
}
```

